Show native views on Wayland: the app's wl_surface as a subsurface below the window, its hole cut by the renderer.

What: `NativeView::new` takes the `wl_proxy` of a role-less `wl_surface` the app made on GPUI's own connection (the
window's display handle), as macOS takes an `NSView`. GPUI gives it a `wp_viewport` at once and, the first frame that
paints the view, a `wl_subsurface` of the window's surface: synchronized, an empty input region (all input stays on
GPUI's surface: patch 0049's `NativeViewInput::Window`). Each frame, before the renderer commits the window, a view
whose placement changed is synchronized, `set_position` and `wp_viewport.set_destination` take the element's bounds
rounded outwards to whole logical pixels (core's `native_view_placement::round_outwards`, 0050), and its surface is
committed (cached until the window's commit), so the move, resize, show or hide lands in the frame that moves the
hole; once the window has committed, the view goes back to desynchronized, so the app's own commits show at once and
never wake GPUI's frame loop. The app attaches and commits its own buffers on its surface. Whenever the shown views or
their order change, the stack is set again in paint order (`place_below(window)` each, frontmost last). A view no
longer painted gets a null buffer, in step with the window's commit, and keeps its role and subsurface (Chromium's
mutter workaround: mutter may never release a buffer whose surface lost its role); painted again, it is shown on that
kept subsurface. At a fractional scale the hole is cut one physical pixel inside the view (core's `hole_inset`,
applied by patch 0047's `set_native_view_hole_inset`), so the window covers the rounded edges. An opaque window's
opaque region leaves out the shown views. A frame whose draw fails places the views back where the window's last
commit shows them, so a retry that commits the old buffer shows them in step. A view clipped away entirely hides; a
view its clip cuts only in part still shows whole until 0054 crops it. Dropping the last `NativeView` handle
destroys the subsurface and viewport. `wl_subcompositor` is bound when offered; without `wl_subcompositor` or
`wp_viewporter`, `NativeView::new` fails as before.

New file `wayland/window/native_view.rs`, ported from chromium/chromium ui/ozone/platform/wayland/host/
wayland_subsurface.cc and wayland_frame_manager.cc @ 4ea2b624ed0c500ec2f23ff17203dff91ad8faa2 (BSD-3-Clause,
Copyright 2020 The Chromium Authors; LICENSE-CHROMIUM, NOTICE, third_party/chromium/PORTED.md, which lists what was
dropped and changed). Table test: `steps` (placement × frame → step). `window.rs` holds only the hooks; `client.rs`
binds the globals. A port, so one patch whatever its size (exempt from the size limit, patch rule 4).

Why: `NativeView::new` failed on every platform but macOS, so a browser shell (wubble) had no zero-overhead page
view on Linux (xuckless/parallax#1). The window's surface and frame commit are private to gpui_linux.

Upstream PR: not filed (filing paused, patch rule 14).

